Loop through each character and counta, e, i, o, u(case-insensitive).

Explanation:We convert the string to lowercase, loop through each character, and count whenever we see a vowel.

The example above checks each vowel with multiple conditions. That works fine, but if you want a more general solution, you can store the vowels in aSetand check characters against it:

Explanation:We first add all vowels to aSet. Then we loop through the characters of the string (converted to lowercase). Each character is checked against the set, and if it exists, the counter goes up. This method is easier to extend if you want to count other characters (like consonants, digits, or symbols).

### Code Snippets

```
Example
String text = "Hello Java";
int count = 0;

for (char c : text.toLowerCase().toCharArray()) {
  if (c == 'a' || c == 'e' || c == 'i' || c == 'o' || c == 'u') {
    count++;
  }
}

System.out.println("Vowels: " + count);
